<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  What survives a new phone, decided rather than inherited (21 set 2026).

  Without this file the platform backs up everything the app owns, which is the
  default and not a choice. Two reasons it had to become one.

  The first is correctness. The Room database is the cached reports and the
  Journal: forecasts with a timestamp on them. Restoring those onto a phone set
  up a week later hands the app hours that have already happened, which
  `WeatherRecency` drops and `WeatherFreshness` marks stale on sight — weight
  carried across a device transfer to be discarded at the first launch.

  The second is that `weather_history` is one row per fetch per place, dated:
  read end to end it is a record of which places the reader watched and when.
  It is the one thing this app holds that a backup has no business copying, and
  it is worth nothing on the other side.

  So an allowlist, not a blocklist: only `files/datastore`, which is where every
  `preferencesDataStore` in `:core:data` writes (`cities` — the saved places —
  plus `settings`, `rules`, `sky`, `sky_alerts`, the four widget stores and the
  rest). Small, and exactly what somebody wants back: their places, their alerts
  and how they set the app up. Everything not named here, the database included,
  is left behind by the fact of not being named.

  The same allowlist twice on purpose. `<cloud-backup>` is the copy that goes to
  Google Drive (end-to-end encrypted with the device's own PIN since Android 9,
  so it is a blob Google cannot read); `<device-transfer>` is the direct
  phone-to-phone move. The two are configured independently, and there is no
  reason to grant one what the other is refused.

  NOT `android:allowBackup="false"`, which would have been the blunter answer:
  on API 31+ it stops the cloud copy but, on devices from some manufacturers,
  leaves device-to-device transfer running anyway. An instruction whose effect
  depends on who made the phone is not a decision, and this file is one.
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <include domain="file" path="datastore" />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <include domain="file" path="datastore" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
